From 02467a3ec5af41165a9fa6d7cf78d7261375d585 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Javier Marcet <javier@marcet.info>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 16:02:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Compute the added_recording windows from the measured
 framerate

The after_start / before_end frame positions bounding the edge
heuristics (delete_show_before_or_after_current / H7,
delete_show_after_last_commercial / H5, and related checks) are
computed from the specified framerate:

    after_start = added_recording * fps * 60;
    before_end  = frame_count - added_recording * fps * 60;

On interlaced H.264 transport streams the container commonly declares
the field rate (50 for 1080i50 PAL broadcasts) while frames actually
decode at 25 fps, and fps is never corrected during the run.  Comskip
already detects and warns about this:

    Frame Rate set to 50.000 f/s
    WARNING: Actual framerate (25.000) different from specified framerate (50.000)

With the default added_recording=14 the end window then covers the last
28 real minutes of the recording instead of 14.  A show whose real
final segment resumes after a long commercial break inside that span is
discarded as if it were the following show:

    H7 Discarding cblock 211 of 1535 seconds because cblock 210 has also logo and small non show gap.

which cuts the whole ending of the recording.

Compute the windows from avg_fps, the framerate measured while
decoding, whenever it diverges from the specified one by more than
1 fps - the same condition the existing framerate warning uses.  When
loading from .txt files avg_fps is set to fps, so behaviour there is
unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Javier Marcet <javier@marcet.info>
---
 comskip.c | 14 ++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/comskip.c b/comskip.c
index dbdace6..5c0ba92 100644
--- a/comskip.c
+++ b/comskip.c
@@ -4358,8 +4358,18 @@ scanagain:
     }
 
 
-    after_start = added_recording * fps * 60;
-    before_end  = frame_count - added_recording * fps * 60;
+    // The specified framerate can be the field rate (e.g. 50 for 1080i50 while
+    // frames decode at 25), which would inflate the added_recording windows.
+    if (avg_fps > 1.0 && fabs(avg_fps - fps) > 1)
+    {
+        after_start = added_recording * avg_fps * 60;
+        before_end  = frame_count - added_recording * avg_fps * 60;
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        after_start = added_recording * fps * 60;
+        before_end  = frame_count - added_recording * fps * 60;
+    }
 
     frame[frame_count].dimCount = 0;
     frame[frame_count].hasBright = 0;
-- 
